Windows Event ID - What It Means - When to Use It
Event ID
What it means
When to use it
4624
Successful logon
Check who logged in, from where, and the logon type
4625
Failed logon
Spot brute force or password guessing
4634
Account logged off
Work out session length
4648
Logon with explicit credentials
Detect use of another account (runas, lateral movement)
4672
Special privileges assigned to a new logon
Spot admin-level logons
4688
New process created
Track programs run (needs process auditing)
4697
Service installed on the system
Detect persistence through services
4720
User account created
Spot unexpected new accounts
4722
User account enabled
Spot re-enabled accounts
4724
Password reset attempt
Detect password changes by someone else
4732
Member added to a local security group
Catch users added to Administrators
4768
Kerberos ticket (TGT) requested
Track domain logons
4769
Kerberos service ticket requested
Detect attacks such as Kerberoasting
4776
Domain controller checked NTLM credentials
Track NTLM logons and failures
1102
Audit log cleared
Strong sign of an attacker hiding traces

Related: Event ID 7045 (System log) records a new service installation.
